WHY AGPL, AND WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT COVER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

AGPL-3.0 was chosen over a permissive licence deliberately. This is software
that gets RUN AS A SERVICE, and AGPL section 13 is the only common licence whose
reciprocity reaches that case: run a modified version where others can use it
over a network, and you must offer them its source. A permissive licence would
allow a modified, closed fork to be operated as a competing service with nothing
given back.

The copyright is held by a single entity, so commercial licensing on different
terms is available on request for anyone whose circumstances make AGPL
unworkable. See CONTRIBUTING.md for why contributions require a copyright
assignment — without it that option disappears permanently.

The AGPL applies to THE CODE IN THIS REPOSITORY.

It does NOT re-license the third-party software this project orchestrates. This
is a control plane: it runs, routes and governs components it does not ship, and
those components carry their own terms. Read this before assuming what you may
do with the stack as a whole — particularly commercially.

The one that most often surprises people:

  n8n — the orchestration runtime this project schedules workflows in.
  n8n is distributed under the SUSTAINABLE USE LICENSE ("fair-code"), which is
  NOT an OSI-approved open-source licence. Among its restrictions, hosting n8n
  and charging others for access is prohibited, and selling a product or service
  whose value substantially depends on n8n requires a separate commercial
  agreement with n8n. Internal use and client work are permitted.
  See: https://docs.n8n.io/sustainable-use-license/

Other components have their own licences, including (non-exhaustive) the vendor
CLIs invoked by the runners (Claude Code, Gemini CLI — governed by their vendors'
terms of service and your subscription), the container base images, and any
Node/Python packages installed at build time.

WHAT THIS MEANS IN PRACTICE

  * Using this code, modifying it, and running it yourself: the AGPL applies to
    our code, and you must still honour each component's own terms.
  * Running a MODIFIED version as a network service: AGPL section 13 requires you
    to offer your users the corresponding source of your modified version.
  * Building a commercial product on the stack AS ASSEMBLED: no licence on this
    repository can grant that on its own, because n8n's terms govern n8n
    regardless of how we license our own work. A commercial licence from us
    covers OUR code only; you would still need n8n's agreement.
  * Replacing n8n with a permissively-licensed orchestrator removes that specific
    constraint. See docs/decisions/ for the analysis.

None of the above is legal advice. If you intend to commercialise anything built
on this stack, have counsel review the component licences — starting with n8n's.
